SELECT 'core-2026-09-v4','modals-ability','87b630c771d79eac83e3be02f06e226cf9455e201ca4d4e9ebd574542396373d',payload || '{"slug":"modals-ability","title":"Ability, possibility and permission: can and could","level":"A2","order":25,"summary":"Use basic modals for ability and permission, and distinguish general past ability from a successful event.","guidance":"Read the form and meaning together. Study the contrasts, then explain a worked example in your own words before starting a fresh practice session.","learningGoals":["A modal keeps the next verb simple","Questions and negatives","General past ability","Permission and polite requests"],"rules":[{"title":"A modal keeps the next verb simple","explanation":"Can and could take a **base verb without to**. They do not take third-person -s. Can commonly describes present ability or informal permission. The surrounding context distinguishes those meanings.","pattern":"She **can swim**. / You **can leave** now.","commonMistakes":["She cans swim and she can to swim are not standard modal constructions."],"examples":["Express ability: “The assistant can **prepare** the visitor guide without help.”","Express ability: “The assistant can **restore** the old portrait without help.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Questions and negatives","explanation":"Move the modal before the subject in a question. Put not after it for a negative. Cannot is normally written as one word; can’t is its common contraction. No do or does is needed.","pattern":"**Can** they help? / They **cannot help**.","commonMistakes":["Do they can help? adds an unnecessary auxiliary."],"examples":["Ask about ability: “**Can** she prepare the visitor guide?”","Ask about ability: “**Can** she restore the old portrait?”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"General past ability","explanation":"Could can describe a general ability in the past. For one successful achievement on a particular occasion, affirmative was/were able to or managed to is often more natural. Could is still possible for perception and some mental processes.","pattern":"As a child, she **could swim**. / Yesterday, she **managed to escape**.","commonMistakes":["Could not can describe failure on one occasion; the restriction mainly concerns affirmative achievement."],"examples":["Use managed to for one successful event: “Yesterday, we managed to **prepare** the visitor guide despite the difficulty.”","Use managed to for one successful event: “Yesterday, we managed to **restore** the old portrait despite the difficulty.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Permission and polite requests","explanation":"Can I asks permission; could I is often more tentative. Could you commonly makes a polite request, not a claim about past ability. Be able to supplies forms unavailable to can, such as will be able to.","pattern":"**Could you help** me? / I’ll **be able to come**.","commonMistakes":["Do not stack two central modals: will can is not standard. Use will be able to."],"examples":["Express future ability with able: “Next month, they will **be able to prepare** the visitor guide without help.”","Express future ability with able: “Next month, they will **be able to restore** the old portrait without help.”"],"skill":"application"}],"tips":[],"source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-ability"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"chapter":{"introduction":["Use basic modals for ability and permission, and distinguish general past ability from a successful event.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"A modal keeps the next verb simple","explanation":"Can and could take a **base verb without to**. They do not take third-person -s. Can commonly describes present ability or informal permission. The surrounding context distinguishes those meanings.","pattern":"She **can swim**. / You **can leave** now.","commonMistakes":["She cans swim and she can to swim are not standard modal constructions."],"examples":["Express ability: “The assistant can **prepare** the visitor guide without help.”","Express ability: “The assistant can **restore** the old portrait without help.”"],"skill":"form","id":"modals-ability-foundation-1","domain":"verbs","referenceIds":["bc-past-ability"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Questions and negatives","explanation":"Move the modal before the subject in a question. Put not after it for a negative. Cannot is normally written as one word; can’t is its common contraction. No do or does is needed.","pattern":"**Can** they help? / They **cannot help**.","commonMistakes":["Do they can help? adds an unnecessary auxiliary."],"examples":["Ask about ability: “**Can** she prepare the visitor guide?”","Ask about ability: “**Can** she restore the old portrait?”"],"skill":"meaning","id":"modals-ability-foundation-2","domain":"verbs","referenceIds":["bc-past-ability"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"General past ability","explanation":"Could can describe a general ability in the past. For one successful achievement on a particular occasion, affirmative was/were able to or managed to is often more natural. Could is still possible for perception and some mental processes.","pattern":"As a child, she **could swim**. / Yesterday, she **managed to escape**.","commonMistakes":["Could not can describe failure on one occasion; the restriction mainly concerns affirmative achievement."],"examples":["Use managed to for one successful event: “Yesterday, we managed to **prepare** the visitor guide despite the difficulty.”","Use managed to for one successful event: “Yesterday, we managed to **restore** the old portrait despite the difficulty.”"],"skill":"contrast","id":"modals-